  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1967-p35.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let the roots be \\(t-d,\\) \\(t,\\) and \\(t+d.\\) Their sum is \\(3t=\\frac{144}{64}=\\frac{9}{4},\\) so \\(t=\\frac{3}{4}.\\) Their product is \\[ t(t^2-d^2)=\\frac{15}{64}. \\] Substituting \\(t=\\frac{3}{4}\\) gives \\(d^2=\\frac{1}{4},\\) so the difference between the extreme roots is \\(2\\lvert d\\rvert=1.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Write the roots as \\(t-d,\\) \\(t,\\) and \\(t+d\\)",
        "Use their sum to find \\(t,\\) then use their product to find \\(d^2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
